Three things this mapping tells you

1. GDS 5 is the hinge. Six things flip at once: abstract games retire, voice becomes primary, fail states vanish, DICE becomes the dashboard's centre, the sundowning response activates, and reminders begin re-routing to the caregiver. If you demo one transition, demo 4 → 5.

2. The problem statement's "cognitive gaming" covers GDS 2–4 only. That's roughly the first third of the journey. Everything after is reminiscence, ambient audio, safety, and caregiver tooling. You're not out of scope — you're honouring "long-term cognitive engagement, emotional well-being" from the closing line, which has no feature letter and which games alone cannot deliver.

3. Your cheapest features have the longest lifespans. Recorded family voices and Culture Pack audio are the only patient-facing things still running at GDS 7 — and they cost you recording time, not engineering. The AR glasses tier, the most expensive thing in the research, is useful for roughly GDS 4–6 and useless at both ends.

